## 3. Standard structure

```text
store-assets/
├── store-assets-workflow.md      # process spec
├── README.md                     # static package overview
├── checking.md                   # optional read-only checker usage and result meanings
├── upgrading.md                  # source evidence and manual compare/merge procedure
├── provenance.json               # installed upstream identity, adaptations, upgrade history
├── {{RULEBOOK_FILE}}             # this document
├── templates/                    # package-level template sources
│   └── store-rulebook.md.template
├── examples/
│   ├── update-tasks.md            # fictional task and field examples; not live records
│   ├── submission-history.md      # snapshot, resubmission, and time examples
│   ├── logo-assets.md             # sourced listing Logo consumers and ownership
│   └── manual-upgrade.md          # fictional customized/unknown-origin upgrade
├── scripts/
│   └── check-store-assets.mjs      # optional single-file Node.js checker; no dependencies
├── {{STORE_PLATFORM}}/
│   ├── asset-rules.md             # Microsoft-specific listing Logo roles and inventory rules
│   ├── current/                  # live published assets, per locale
│   ├── release-prep/             # draft assets, per locale
│   ├── submissions/              # one record file per submission
│   └── templates/                # includes Microsoft asset-inventory.json.template
├── apple-app-store/              # reserved skeleton, future Adapter
│   ├── current/
│   ├── release-prep/
│   ├── submissions/
│   └── templates/
└── google-play/                  # reserved skeleton, future Adapter
    ├── current/
    ├── release-prep/
    ├── submissions/
    └── templates/
```

The lifecycle directories are shared across store adapters; each adapter keeps its own fields, templates, and validation rules. Microsoft-specific asset rules and inventory templates apply only to `microsoft-store`. `apple-app-store/` and `google-play/` exist as **empty reserved skeletons** only — no rules for them are assumed until researched and verified.

`current/`, `release-prep/`, and `submissions/` are created by first initialization; locale subfolders are created when a locale is actually used.

The installed `provenance.json` records upstream evidence, local adaptations and upgrade history following `upgrading.md`. Preserve it when bringing in a newer kit. Its upstream template versions, this document's generation/local marker, toolkit release, application version and package version are distinct. Unknown original provenance remains unknown. Merge later template changes into this rulebook deliberately, preserving local extensions and generation evidence; do not regenerate over an existing customized document. Toolkit upgrades preserve every store's current/candidate/history files and leave any needed content migrations as separate work.

## 4. Directory responsibilities

### `current/`

Represents the live store state, organized per locale:

```text
current/<locale>/
├── listing.md
├── release-notes.md
└── screenshots/
    ├── source/
    └── export/
```

- Must not be edited while preparing an update.
- Must not be replaced until the user confirms the new submission is live.
- If a newer publication is confirmed but promotion is unfinished, this remains the last archived baseline; report the archive gap instead of claiming it matches the latest live state.
- Older published versions are preserved through retained Git history. Each submitted attempt also records its immutable commit/path reference; today's `current/` path is not a historical snapshot identifier.

### `release-prep/`

Contains the complete candidate assets for one upcoming submission, per locale, mirroring `current/<locale>/` so publish is a tree-level replacement.

```text
release-prep/<locale>/
├── listing.md
├── release-notes.md
└── screenshots/
    ├── source/
    └── export/
```

- Prepare updates only here, never in `current/`.
- Only one active submission may exist for this store adapter. Do not begin another release preparation while a submission is `Draft`, `Submitted`, `In certification`, or `Action required`.
- A preparation and its independent Draft record are one deliverable. Resume an existing task through its record; never create a second active record to continue it.
- Inspect candidate content before copying. A candidate without a matching active record requires the workflow's recovery procedure; do not overwrite or delete it to start again.
- Each affected locale must be a complete snapshot of the intended live state, including any removals.
- The submission variables live in the submission record (in `submissions/`).
- The status field tracks the whole flow; no per-folder naming needed.
- Use the same tree whether the update is a new build or only listing metadata and screenshots.
- For a listing-only update, preserve the confirmed application version and unrelated assets/release notes. Record the asset changes in the submission, without inventing an application release.

### Microsoft Store Logo ownership

For new/revised Logos, follow `microsoft-store/asset-rules.md` and `examples/logo-assets.md` at kit root. Add `asset-inventory.json` plus `logos/source/` and `logos/export/` under the affected locale or `shared/` in each state as needed. The Microsoft template is `microsoft-store/templates/asset-inventory.json.template`; it keeps unknown inputs explicit until resolved.

The inventory binds each export to its source, role, language consumers, actual destination, dimensions/format, transparency declaration, generation method, requirements source/date, and visual review evidence. A shared repository file still has language-specific upload consumers. Preserve screenshot source/export conventions. Package icons remain application-project evidence, distinct from listing uploads.

An affected shared tree is a complete candidate scope alongside the complete affected locale trees. Include it in Submitted files and every corresponding frozen attempt. Preserve unchanged shared consumers. On promotion, rebase only inventory `source`/`export` path prefixes from `release-prep/` to `current/`; the approved image/source bytes and original historical references stay intact. Existing projects without inventories require deliberate review and no automatic migration.

### `submissions/`

One record file per submission:

```text
submissions/<submission-id>.md
```

Records repository submission ID, date, released and candidate application versions, update type, new-package applicability/evidence, optional store-issued ID, locales, status, publication date, changes, file references, open items, and review notes. Git history preserves versions, so records do not duplicate all assets.

The same record also holds numbered submission attempts and sourced events. Preserve these when a review returns for fixes or a later publication replaces `current/`. Keep the referenced commits reachable in retained application history; a hash written in Markdown alone does not retain a Git object.

`Submission ID` identifies this repository record. A store-issued ID, if known, belongs in `Store submission ID`; never copy the local ID into that field as if it came from the store. The date in the filename is not evidence of actual submission.

### `templates/`

Two template locations, kept apart on purpose:

- `templates/` (package level) — the template source that generates this rulebook. The package `README.md` is static, not generated.
- `<STORE_PLATFORM>/templates/` — store-specific templates; Microsoft ships an asset inventory template. The file skeletons in §6 remain the required source for listing, release-notes, and submission-record formats.

Use the §6 file skeletons instead of inventing a new structure.

## 5. Naming rules

- Directories: lowercase `kebab-case` (`store-assets`, `microsoft-store`, `release-prep`); locales use official codes (`en-US`, `zh-CN`).
- Markdown files: short lowercase `kebab-case` names (`listing.md`, `release-notes.md`, `2026-08-04-listing-refresh.md`).
- Every level-1 heading includes the product name:
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Listing — {{LOCALE}}`
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Release Notes — {{LOCALE}}`
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Submission — {{SUBMISSION_ID}}`
- Screenshots: two-digit order + semantic lowercase `kebab-case`, no spaces, no version words (`final`, `new`, `latest`):

  ```text
  01-primary-value.png
  02-core-workflow.png
  ```

  Editable files go in `<state>/<locale>/screenshots/source/`; upload-ready files in `<state>/<locale>/screenshots/export/`, where `<state>` is `current` or `release-prep`.

- Listing Logo source/export files use semantic lowercase kebab-case names in the inventory's owned `logos/source/` and `logos/export/` directories. Generate only derivatives with confirmed consumers; do not infer a size matrix from Windows package icons.

