### `submissions/<submission-id>.md` ({{STORE_PLATFORM}})

```markdown
# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Submission — {{SUBMISSION_ID}}

Submission ID:
Draft created date:
Submission date:
Released application version:
Application version:
Update type:
New package version:
Package evidence:
Store submission ID:
Locales:
Status:
Publication date:
Publication confirmed date:

## Changes

## Submitted files

## Submission attempts

## Events

## Open items

## Certification result

## Review notes
```

Field rules for a new or resumed preparation:

- `Update type`: `listing-only` for store content/assets without a new application package; `package-update` for a new package, including a mixed package-and-listing update. These labels are local workflow terms.
- `Released application version`: confirmed published version for this task.
- `Application version`: same released version for `listing-only`; the confirmed application-project target or `Pending — <reason>` for `package-update`.
- `New package version` and `Package evidence`: `Not applicable` for `listing-only`; actual package version and application-project evidence references for `package-update`, or `Pending — <reason>` during incomplete preparation. These fields concern the new package, not whether the already published app has a package.
- Package evidence identifies the relevant artifact/build/QA sources without copying binaries, credentials, or signing material here. Product version, package version, toolkit release, and this rulebook's template version are distinct values.
- `Store submission ID`: the actual external ID when known; otherwise `Pending — not reported by the store`. Lack of an external ID alone does not prevent preparation or readiness.
- `Draft created date`: known date this local record was created, derived from its draft-created event. Do not use it as a store date or as proof of when an orphan candidate was originally prepared.
- `Submission date`: known occurrence date of the latest actual submitted attempt; leave empty before submission or if that attempt's date is unknown. Earlier dates remain in Events.
- `Publication date`: evidenced platform publication date only. `Publication confirmed date`: date explicit user go-live confirmation was received. Published status with an empty platform date is valid when only confirmation is known.
- `Submitted files`: label paths as candidate files while Draft; list their intended roles and planned additions/removals. A path is not proof that it was uploaded, reviewed, or approved.
- For checkable file references, use one bullet with a backtick-quoted kit-relative path or Markdown link per path; a directory reference explicitly includes descendants. Bare legacy paths without spaces are also recognized. Unstructured prose requires review. Snapshot paths below remain relative to the application Git root, not this record or the kit.
- `Open items`: list missing facts, incomplete candidate files, checks, and user decisions with the affected action. Use `None` only when no open items remain.

Do not carry literal `{{...}}` tokens into an actual submission or listing. Use the allowed pending values for unknown facts and `Not applicable` only when the task type makes a field inapplicable. A package-update cannot pass readiness with pending target version or package evidence; a listing-only update is not blocked by absent new-package fields marked not applicable.

Older records may use free-text labels such as `new version` or `listing refresh` and omit the new fields. Preserve historical records. When resuming an old preparation, establish its actual type from confirmed intent/evidence, retain the old label in Review notes if changing it, and add only known facts or explicit gaps. Never infer a new package from the old label alone. See `examples/update-tasks.md` in the package root for fictional examples.

### Submission attempt format

Under `Submission attempts`, add an entry only for an approved snapshot or a reported actual submission whose snapshot is still pending. Use increasing local attempt numbers. A prepared entry alone does not prove upload/submission. For an ordinary incomplete Draft this section can say `None — not frozen or submitted`.

```text
### Attempt 1
Snapshot commit:
Snapshot paths:
-
Approval source:
Snapshot verification:
Verified at:
```

- `Snapshot commit`: the full commit object ID resolved from the application's Git repository; otherwise `Pending — <reason>`. Do not fix its length to one Git hash algorithm. Store the resolved ID rather than HEAD, a branch/tag name, or an abbreviation.
- `Snapshot paths`: literal, forward-slash, repository-root-relative paths at that commit covering complete affected candidate trees. They must not escape that repository. For pending/unlocated historical assets, state the gap instead of inventing paths. Current working paths are useful navigation only.
- `Approval source`: reference the actual approval and its scope; use `Pending — <reason>` when evidence of earlier approval is unavailable. Do not manufacture an approval to document an already reported store action.
- `Snapshot verification`: `Verified` or `Pending — <reason>`, covering commit/path/content availability and the match to approved content. `Verified at` is the actual verification time, or `Unknown` if not verified. A historical verification is not a promise that objects will remain available forever; reverify on use.
- A snapshot covers the recorded repository assets, not an external package artifact merely referenced by filename. Keep package/build/hash evidence separate and truthful.
- Do not replace a submitted attempt's snapshot when fixing assets. Add the next attempt; retain earlier entries. Corrections must preserve the prior value and evidence of what changed.

### Event format and time semantics

Under `Events`, use increasing event IDs. Add a draft-created event when creating a new record; do not prefill events for actions that have not happened.

```text
### Event 1
Event: draft-created
Attempt: Not applicable
Occurred at:
Occurred timezone:
Observed at:
Recorded at:
Source:
Notes:
```

- `Event`: `draft-created`, `submitted`, `status-observed`, `draft-reopened`, `platform-published`, `go-live-confirmed`, `promoted`, or `correction`. `draft-reopened` records the local return to Draft for confirmed review fixes, with its authorization/source; it is not a store verdict. Record observed status and any external ID in Notes where relevant; do not invent intermediate events.
- For a machine-readable `status-observed` event, write `Notes: Status: In certification; source limitations` using the actual reported status. Unstructured older notes remain reviewable evidence; do not rewrite them to imply an unobserved transition.
- `Attempt`: the relevant local attempt number; `Not applicable` for record-level events; `Unknown` only when the association itself is not established and is listed as an open item.
- `Occurred at`: when the action actually happened, as supported by the source. Use an ISO 8601 timestamp with explicit UTC offset, a date-only `YYYY-MM-DD` if that is the known precision, or `Unknown`.
- `Occurred timezone`: timezone/UTC offset for a date-only occurrence when known, or `Unknown`; full timestamps already carry their offset. Never turn a known date into midnight or assume a source's timezone.
- `Observed at`: when the event information was received/observed. `Recorded at`: when this entry was written. Preserve their different meanings, known precision, and offsets; allow `Unknown` for unavailable earlier observations. Current recording time is known, not evidence of an unknown occurrence time.
- `Source`: a verifiable file/report reference or a concise dated user-report/approval reference. State when this is user-reported rather than independently observed. Do not copy private conversation or credentials into records.
- `Notes`: reported status/scope, precision or evidence limitations, and correction references as applicable. A correction identifies its earlier entry, old/new values, and reason; retain the original evidence.

Summary dates derive from these events, in the timezone of the evidenced date; observation/recording dates never fill unknown platform/submission occurrence dates. `go-live-confirmed` records receipt of explicit user confirmation, not an inferred earlier publication time. A later correction can clarify a summary without rewriting the earlier evidence.

For legacy records, do not silently reinterpret `Submission date` or `Publication date`: the latter may previously have meant user confirmation. Preserve the old value and explain its known/unknown meaning in Review notes, then add evidence/correction events as facts become available. Missing history stays an open item; a convenient current snapshot must not be labeled as the past submitted assets. See `examples/submission-history.md` in the package root.

## 7. Content accuracy rules

Write and update claims only against verifiable sources: PRD, released application behavior, release notes, QA, approved screenshots, and confirmed user decisions. Never advertise:

- unreleased features
- unsupported formats or platforms
- unimplemented AI or agent integrations
- privacy guarantees outside the application's control
- performance claims without evidence

Unverifiable claims are flagged for review, not silently kept. No invented marketing copy, legal claims, prices, release dates, or capabilities.

For a package-update Draft, copy may describe confirmed implemented target behavior while package evidence is still pending, but it must remain a candidate with that readiness gap. Product plans alone are not implementation evidence. A listing-only update describes the already released application; neither task permits unverified claims to be presented as ready or live.

## 8. Security rules

Never store in the repository:

- store account passwords, API keys, or recovery codes
- signing certificates or payment information
- secret environment variables
- private documents or personal identification
- unredacted account screenshots or private user data

Screenshots containing personal names, account information, private paths, or confidential documents must be redacted before commit.

## 9. Ready-for-submission checklist

A release preparation is ready only when:

- [ ] The candidate has its own record, ID, confirmed task type, affected locales, and change scope; there is no conflicting active submission or unowned candidate content.
- [ ] Released/candidate application versions and new-package fields follow the task-type rules above; no version or evidence is invented.
- [ ] For `package-update`, target version, exact package version, and relevant package/build/QA evidence are known and reviewed; for `listing-only`, new-package fields are `Not applicable`.
- [ ] Each affected locale is a complete intended snapshot; unchanged files are preserved and planned additions/removals are recorded.
- [ ] Candidate file references exist and match the record's scope; required open items are resolved, with any unverified external result stated explicitly.
- [ ] Required listing fields are complete.
- [ ] Listing-only copy matches the released product; package-update copy matches verified target-package behavior, with no speculative features.
- [ ] Keywords are relevant, not over-repeated.
- [ ] Release notes present when required; a listing-only update preserves existing application release notes without inventing a new release.
- [ ] Screenshots are final and upload-ready.
- [ ] New/revised Logos have a complete inventory, verified package/product/device applicability, source provenance, named upload fields and language consumers, and reviewed source/export files; unresolved legacy Logo mappings remain visible gaps.
- [ ] Logo technical checks and visual approval are reviewed separately. PNG headers do not prove alpha/background appearance, upload success, or that a live rendering problem is resolved.
- [ ] Shared sources/exports and their inventories are included in complete candidate/snapshot scopes; all consumers of changed shared exports are in the record's Locales.
- [ ] Screenshot order is confirmed.
- [ ] Filenames follow the naming rules in §5.
- [ ] No unsupported feature is advertised.
- [ ] No sensitive information included.
- [ ] Store-specific requirements checked.
- [ ] User has reviewed the final package.
- [ ] Before submission handoff, the approved complete candidate is bound to a verified full commit/path snapshot in its attempt; preparation alone is not an actual submitted event.
- [ ] Event sources and time meanings are explicit; unknown external times and unsupported legacy interpretations remain unknown. A source field alone does not prove external publication.

The optional `scripts/check-store-assets.mjs` reports structural errors, review items, and informational findings using this format. Its command/runtime/exit-code contract lives in `checking.md` at package root. It never repairs files or proves external facts; a zero exit code does not replace this checklist. Unsupported legacy/custom formats require deliberate review.

